package instar:text@0.1.0;

// Instar's text capability, as a package of its own rather than more surface
// on `instar:kernel`.
//
// The kernel world is guest *lifecycle*: events, operations, cancellation, and
// the UI commit. Text is an optional host service a guest may or may not use,
// and folding it into the kernel would start a list — text-buffer, text-view,
// image, audio-graph, gpu-surface, file-handle — that ends with one giant
// runtime API instead of a kernel plus capabilities.
//
// `instar-kernel` assembles the world and generates the bindings; it does not
// semantically own this interface. `instar-text`, the Rust crate that
// implements the text model, does not know this file exists: it has no
// Wasmtime, no WIT, and no idea a guest is involved. Putting `text.wit` there
// would gradually turn "the text model" into "the guest ABI adapter for text".

interface text-types {
    /// Why a text resource could not be created.
    ///
    /// Deliberately not a generic error string: each variant is a bound the
    /// host enforces, and a guest that hits one should be able to tell which.
    variant text-error {
        /// The live-buffer ceiling was reached. A bound on *live* resources,
        /// not on historical ones -- slots are reused.
        too-many-buffers(u32),
        /// The live-view ceiling was reached.
        too-many-views(u32),
        /// The handle named a resource this generation cannot reach: it was
        /// dropped, or it belonged to a generation that no longer exists.
        no-such-resource,
        /// The host could not answer -- it is shutting down, or the thread
        /// that owns the text subsystem is gone.
        unavailable,
    }
}

/// Host-owned text resources, leased to a guest as capabilities.
///
/// # These are leases, not documents
///
/// A `text-buffer` handle is a capability naming a buffer the *host* owns on
/// the thread that owns presentation. The rope, its revisions and its journal
/// are never in the guest's resource table, and never in its linear memory.
/// Dropping a handle ends the guest's ability to name a document; whether the
/// document itself ends is the host's decision, which is what makes guest
/// restart survivable later.
///
/// # Synchronous here, async in the host
///
/// None of these is a WIT `async func`. Declaring one would opt into Component
/// Model async semantics -- overlapping subtasks and a different ABI -- when
/// what is actually wanted is the opposite: the guest suspends inside the call
/// while the host's Rust future awaits the thread that owns the text
/// subsystem. `bindgen!`'s `imports: { default: async | trappable }` is that
/// distinction, and it needs nothing from the WIT.
interface text {
    use text-types.{text-error};

    resource text-buffer;
    resource text-view;

    /// Opens an empty document.
    ///
    /// Empty, and only empty, in this package. Creating a buffer from guest
    /// contents raises canonical transfer, copy limits, bootstrap revisions
    /// and resynchronization all at once -- which is Package C, and pulling it
    /// forward here would mean designing synchronization inside a resource
    /// constructor.
    create-empty-buffer: func() -> result<text-buffer, text-error>;

    /// Opens a view onto a buffer.
    ///
    /// Borrowed, because a view does not take ownership of the document: many
    /// views may name one buffer, and the buffer outlives any of them.
    create-view: func(buffer: borrow<text-buffer>) -> result<text-view, text-error>;
}
